A||||20||FieroBert||FieroBert Recovered - BUT...||||9||
Z||000000||FieroBert||07-24-2001||08:20 PM||||Got a call from the Mesa, Az Police today that my 86 GT was recovered in Phoenix,  however the wheels were missing.  Went to the towing company lot and it looked pitifull just "laying" on the ground.  Indeed the wheels were missing along with the stereo, amplifier and speakers.  The body and paint didn't look to bad but covered with fingerprint ink.  Did not have my keys with me and the battery is either missing or dead so I could not open the rear cover to see if anything in the engine compartment is missing.<P>Questions though - what would be damaged with the car sitting on the ground?  i.e. what is the lowest point of the car, especially in the rear.  I'm wondering if the car is sitting on the oil pan.  The front appears to be sitting on the brake discs. I'm worried that the towing company may have caused more physical damage to the car than the thieves did!<P>The insurance adjusters first statement was that it is probably totaled.  That would be a big waste of a fairly good Fiero.  Will keep you posted of their decision, may be a good parting out candidate.<P>Thanks for everyone's support through all of this.  It feels good to have not been alone, knowing that there are so many people I have never met that actually care.  What a find this forum has been.  Again my sincere thanks!||||reg||9
Z||000001||loafer87gt||07-24-2001||08:30 PM||||Glad to hear you got it back. I can remember when I got a call when the police found my car after the first time it was stolen. It's not nice to say, but I almost wish the police hadn't found the car. The guys had used a crowbar to pry the back trunk open, trashing the wing, and smoked something with the front of the car cracking my front fascia. The inside was also trashed, and the guys toasted my new clutch in the car and the shop wouldn't warranty it because of the theft. After getting the clutch fixed, after only about hundred kilometers of driving the bearings went on my engine. The car has never been the same since, and has been sitting out in a compound since. The insurance guys said the clutch and engine were coincidences. *SIGH* I would take your car to a mechanic and have him listen to the engine for any odd noises, and to give the car a good look over before you talk to the insurance guys. Here's hoping you have better luck with your car.||||reg||12
Z||000002||87GTBro||07-24-2001||08:34 PM||||If the police match the fingerprints and "accidently" give you the names of the jerks who did this... Make sure you give us a holler!  We all want a swing at the pinata!<P>I don't think you have to worry about the oil pan.  The engine cradle is slightly below it... I think.<P>Why would they do such a thing anyway!?  Those werent high-dollar rims were they?<P>Anyway... glad you got it back...  collect the "totalled" insurance money and restore it!!!  Hope they give you more than book value for it.  If they catch the guys who did this, make sure you get revenge!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||07-24-2001||08:36 PM||||"clutch and engine were coincidences. *SIGH*" <P>WHAT EVER.  <P>I agree, if my car was ever stolen I hope it was never found.   BUT wait I don't have fire or theft on my car so, nevermind.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Urchin||07-24-2001||09:22 PM||||Hopefully you are able to get it on the road again.  That'd be a tough way to lose a your car. Good luck buddy ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Galen||07-24-2001||09:56 PM||||I'm with 87GTBro.. collect the money and restore  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"It may be the opinion of the court, but its still wrong"</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/download.html" TARGET=_blank>Download FieroProject.com Chat Client v1.50 Today</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierochic88||07-24-2001||11:20 PM||||Glad to hear it's recovered.  Please keep us posted!<P>Jennifer||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||07-24-2001||11:32 PM||||Check the coolant tubes and fual tank. those are the most likely thing to trash jacking it or setting it on a rock. Otherwise not much of an issue to be on the ground.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrgone||07-25-2001||03:18 AM||||oh man, so much stuff could be dmaged.<BR>think about it, they know nothing about fieros and they drove like maniacs, jacked it up, and plopped it on the ground. your clutch is prolly toast.<P>im just speaking from what i would do if i had a car that i was trashing. 6000rpm powershifting, i would dump the clutch at 5grand a few times, see how fast it goes in reverse, maybe jump it etc. <P>you also may be in for a surprise when you get the hoods open.<P>sorry to hear this.<p>[This message has been edited by mrgone (edited 07-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Haze_Performance||07-25-2001||03:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroBert:<BR><B><BR>Questions though - what would be damaged with the car sitting on the ground?  i.e. what is the lowest point of the car, especially in the rear.  I'm wondering if the car is sitting on the oil pan.  The front appears to be sitting on the brake discs. I'm worried that the towing company may have caused more physical damage to the car than the thieves did!<P>thanks!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Glad ya got it back... Heres my 2 2/3 cents:<P>Like theogre said - Check the coolant tubes and fuel tank. But I think you throw some wheels on it & bring it to a local muffler shop. They should put it up for free & you can give everything a close inspection. I would be more concerned about the Police tow guys more than the thieves. The Tow guys probably just tied a cable to the car & pulled it up onto the truck. (They have no respect for peoples cars. Those dirty !*$@#) That might cause more damage than just pulling the wheels off & droppin it.<P>Good Luck. I hope all is OK. Keep us updated..<P>Haze  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/indyclick.gif">  <BR></A><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Click above to visit my website.</B></FONT></FONT>||||reg||10
Z||000010||rogergarrison||07-25-2001||04:07 AM||||try to get the most money you can if they total it. show em saved reciepts for stuff youve had done, especially expensive stuff. Theyll sell it back to you for a couple of hundred dollars. In a few cases I know, they came out way ahead by having it stolen. One girl got hers back, paid off the loan balance, I fixed all the damage, and she made $2000 to boot.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||07-25-2001||06:13 AM||||Unfortunately, the insurance thing is something I actually know about.  My last '85 GT was totalled by the insurance company.  There really wasn't that much wrong with it so I did alright, but...unless you have additional coverage with the insurance co., you probably won't get any extra money for add ons such as a new stereo, fancy wheels, etc and they won't give you a dime extra for new parts (like a new clutch or motor).  Book value is all you'll get, I'm sorry to say.  The up side to this is that you can generally buy the car back for 10% of book value.  That's what I did.  Turned out well for me as I was able to sell the car and buy another one (wanted a fastback this time).  Best of luck to you.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tigger||07-25-2001||08:22 AM||||As the others have said, check the tank, coolant tubes, and brake lines.  The only thing I know for sure that would be damaged is the rock shields behind the rotors.  Take a pair of vise grips with you and bend those back when you get it jacked back up to put the wheels on.<P>If you need wheels, post a thread in the mall section.  I'm sure someone here would have a set to offer.<P>Glad they found it in sort of one piece.  <P>Good Luck, hope you can get it back on the road soon...||||reg||1
Z||000013||JSocha||07-25-2001||09:17 AM||||I don't know what the laws are in your state regarding totaling and/or vehicle registration.<P>Just food for thought, if the insurance company totals your car, you buy it back, get it running, won't you now have to have a "Salvaged Title" on the car now?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||88CoupeV6||07-25-2001||10:17 AM||||Check ebay for your rims, stereo equip. The may try selling it there. You never know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||3
Z||000015||firstfiero||07-25-2001||10:17 AM||||Thats why my car is still one the road. My friend who owned it befor me wrecked it up pretty bad. The insurance company said they wanted to total it .He told them give me the max that you give and I'll worry about the rest....<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||fierohoho||07-25-2001||10:59 AM||||Great to hear you got your car back.<P>If the insurance company totals your car you can "buy" it back from them and put it back on the road and don't let them screw you, they are out for $ first and you come in second.<P>If they total the car your ace in the hole is the vehicle title in your hand, I don't believe they can do much to dispose of the car without it.<P>As for the SCUM who took it, go to the main office for the police departments involved and get copies of the reports. <P>They will give you them most likely for a small fee.<P>Check for a Crime Victims Reparation Board in your area, the cops should know how to contact one or where you can call, they "may" have a little cash for you.<P>When you pick up the reports find out who the investigating officer is so you can call him to keep updated on the case, and, ask to be notified of any arrests.<P>Ask if they will check local pawn shops for the missing stereo items.<P>Give them any identifying info on the missing items like sratches,dents or stuff like that. Serial #?<P>If they make an arrest contact the prosecuting attorney and talk to him about what will happen to the DIRTBAGS.<P>Take pictures of the car from all angles and document the damage to the car whether from the tow guys or the LOW LIFE MOUTH BREATHERS WHO RIDE THE SHORT BUS.<P>Offer before and after pictures to the prosecuting attorney along with repair estimates, ask for restitution as part of sentencing.<P>Supply the attorney with a list of missing items and there value if the police didn't already ask.<P>Sorry, didn't mean to ramble, just wanted to help.<P>Death to all Fiero thieves! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000017||Butter||07-25-2001||11:02 AM||||Glad to hear you have it back.<P>I would check all those things previously mentioned for damage.<P>Then I would advertise discretly for any of the missing parts that I thought might still be floating around.  Check the pawn shops and such.  <P>Now round hear we normally take care of the perpitraiter ourselves when possible.  Coarse 911 is probally the folks to call.<P>In Hillbilly terms 911 means fire 9 shots at him leave 1 in the clip and 1 in the chamber while you reload.  Where you fire those shots is relative to how you feel about the damage caused.  The last thief I talked with I figured could be trusted got a piece of lead up his a$$ as he was jumping threw a window with someone else's stereo.  He said that ended his thieving carreer plus he had to go to court and make resitution.  But Don't go to a gun fight with a knife.  The best weapon you will always find is on your shoulders.  Keep that in good order and you just might find some justice.   ||||reg||1
Z||000018||rogergarrison||07-25-2001||12:28 PM||||Well, I have State Farm (worse in the business) and theyve paid me extra for extra stuff I had reciepts for prorated. I had a van that was stripped. the stuff not covered like tv and vcr were all covered by them too, but under my homeowners since they werent considered 'normal' automotive equip.<BR>got paid value, plus aftrmrkt stereo, wheels tires, my engine work, trailer hitch, custom seats, and the custom paint job.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Ken Wittlief||07-25-2001||02:02 PM||||when it comes time to decide if you want to rebuild the car, or scrap it, in the end it all comes down to one thing.<P> almost everything we do, we do for emotional reasons, to feel a certain way, or to avoid feeling a certain way.<P>  if you go and rebuilt the car, and everytimg you drive it all you can think of is how screwed up it is now compaired to the way it was, or if its a constant reminder of the way it was trashed, then you are better off bailing on the car.<P> if you take pride in being able to turn a bad situation into something better, even if the car is never as good as it was, and you will be proud of yourself for putting the machine back into one piece, then rebuilding the car will be something that will make you smile everytime you drive it.<P>  only you can decide how you will feel in the end if you let it go and find another car to start fresh with, or if you pull this one up out of the mud and restore it.||||reg||10
Z||000020||Jake2m4||07-26-2001||09:25 AM||||Let the insurance total it.  Buy it back.  Fix it or sell it.  Either way you should be able to do well.  Glad you got it back.<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
